AI has changed the ways which game levels, environments and interactivity are designed in the video games industries. More strategically integrated in the concept of Procedural Content Generation (PCG), AI is now a key approach to constructing interesting, complex and dynamic game environments. As algorithm-based map generation can be limitless and varied, the amount of work for developers is minimized, while the fun is greatly extended for people playing games. 
AI can enhance the design of games at both the strategic and technical levels which opens a great deal of new avenues for future game development. This research builds from theories in Experience-Driven Reinforcement Learning (EDRL), where AI systems generate new levels based on player’s interaction while changing the difficulty and gaming experiences for better results according to the intended user. 
For example, EDRL has shown in how it has been used to create new levels for the traditional video game, Super Mario Bros., highlighting what AI is capable of as far as both the process of providing greater entertainment of users and improving the organizational structure of the game is concerned. [1]
